  "solution": {
    "approach": "The parity of an integer can be determined by checking the least significant bit. If N % 2 == 0 (or (N & 1) == 0) the number is even, otherwise it is odd. For each test case we read N, evaluate the condition and output the corresponding string. This runs in O(1) per test case.",
    "timeComplexity": "O(T) total, O(1) per test case.",
    "spaceComplexity": "O(1) additional space.",
    "code": {
      "C": "",
      "C++": "",
      "Java": "import java.io.*;\nimport java.util.*;\n\npublic class Main {\n    public static void main(String[] args) throws IOException {\n        BufferedReader br = new BufferedReader(new InputStreamReader(System.in));\n        StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder();\n        int T = Integer.parseInt(br.readLine().trim());\n        for (int i = 0; i < T; i++) {\n            long N = Long.parseLong(br.readLine().trim());\n            sb.append((N & 1) == 0 ? \"true\" : \"false\");\n            if (i + 1 < T) sb.append('\\n');\n        }\n        System.out.print(sb.toString());\n    }\n}",
      "Python3": "",
      "JavaScript": ""
    },
